user_prompt:
Below is a work record written by an author who developed a campaign idea for a client. Judge the author's personality as it shows in HOW they worked and wrote: what they noticed, how they weighed options, what risks they worried about, the tone they used. Do not judge the quality of the idea.

Rate how strongly each Big Five trait is expressed by the author, from 1 (very low) to 9 (very high), 5 = average or no clear signal.
- Openness to Experience
- Conscientiousness
- Extraversion
- Agreeableness
- Neuroticism

Work record:
{
 "task_understanding": {
  "focus": "The first paycheck is a huge emotional milestone, but it is usually documented through a number on a screen or a dull payslip. Canva's opportunity is to own the moment that comes before the money: the first thing someone made that someone else valued enough to pay for. That links a universal life event to Canva's core truth, that creating is how people become professionals, and it reaches the freelancers, small business owners, students and new hires who make up Canva's growth base.",
  "assumptions": [
   "Canva is widely seen as a friendly, democratizing design tool, and is under-recognized as the place where first professional work gets made and paid for.",
   "The category (B2B SaaS and productivity) communicates through efficiency and features. Emotional, human storytelling is a differentiator here.",
   "The audience spans first-time employees, freelancers, side-hustlers, solo founders and the managers or HR teams who onboard them. Many of them already made something in Canva to earn that money.",
   "People remember the work and the feeling of their first paycheck far better than the amount. The amount can also trigger comparison, shame or privacy concerns.",
   "The first paycheck means different things across cultures and life stages (a gift to parents, proof of independence, a first rent payment), so the idea must be flexible rather than tied to one story.",
   "A social, user-generated campaign will work only if the participation format is extremely easy to make and share, which suits Canva's template model."
  ]
 },
 "candidates": [
  {
   "id": "C1",
   "title": "Welcome to Payday: the HR Moment Kit",
   "core_idea": "Treat the first payday as an onboarding moment employers can design. Canva gives HR and managers a 'First Payday' kit: a team-signed card, a short celebration visual, and a 'what your first month of work produced' recap that arrives alongside the first payslip.",
   "brand_connection": "Speaks directly to Canva's B2B and team-collaboration positioning. Shows Canva Teams, brand kits and shared workspaces as tools for culture, not just slides.",
   "expected_strengths": [
    "Clear B2B relevance and a direct path to team and enterprise adoption.",
    "Easy to turn into templates, a landing page and HR-community content.",
    "Warm, human angle for a category that is usually dry."
   ],
   "main_concerns": [
    "Less culturally resonant with the public, so weak earned-media potential.",
    "Reaches employees only through employers, and excludes freelancers and creators.",
    "Risks feeling like a corporate gimmick if the payday gesture is not sincere."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "C2",
   "title": "The First Invoice",
   "core_idea": "For freelancers and side-hustlers, the first paycheck begins with an invoice they design themselves. Canva reframes the invoice as a professional coming-out moment, with a 'First Invoice' template series and a ritual of sending it with confidence.",
   "brand_connection": "Builds on Canva's functional role in small business: invoices, proposals, quotes and brand identity. A straightforward product demonstration with an emotional wrapper.",
   "expected_strengths": [
    "Concrete product utility with measurable template usage.",
    "Speaks to a large, growing freelancer and solopreneur audience.",
    "Easy to support with creator partnerships and tutorials."
   ],
   "main_concerns": [
    "Competes with dedicated invoicing tools and can look like a feature push.",
    "Narrow audience that excludes employees getting a first salary.",
    "Emotional upside is limited; invoices are not an inherently shareable object."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "C3",
   "title": "The Work Behind the First Paycheck",
   "core_idea": "Nobody remembers the number, but everyone remembers what they made to earn it: the bakery menu, the wedding invitation, the first client deck, the logo for a friend's startup. Canva invites people to publish a 'First Paid' receipt, pairing the design that earned their first money with a blurred amount, the date and who paid them. The campaign turns first paychecks into a public gallery of the work that started careers.",
   "brand_connection": "Positions Canva as the place where first professional work is born and makes 'Made in Canva' a mark of a career beginning rather than a watermark. It celebrates the user's creation, not the tool.",
   "expected_strengths": [
    "Surfaces a fresh, revealing insight: the work is more memorable than the money.",
    "Highly shareable, user-generated format with built-in nostalgia and pride.",
    "Works across freelancers, employees, founders and students, and can scale to different cultures.",
    "Produces authentic proof of Canva's role in real livelihoods, which is ideal for B2B credibility."
   ],
   "main_concerns": [
    "Needs a strong seed of stories at launch to avoid looking empty.",
    "Amounts and payers raise privacy and comparison issues.",
    "Not everyone's first paycheck came from a design, so the premise could feel exclusive."
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "C4",
   "title": "The First Paycheck Index",
   "core_idea": "Canva publishes an annual data-storytelling project on what a first paycheck bought, in hours worked, across cities and generations. It is built from a public survey and visualized in open Canva templates anyone can remix to tell their own version.",
   "brand_connection": "Demonstrates Canva's power in data visualization and storytelling, giving journalists, teams and educators open templates to make their own data stories.",
   "expected_strengths": [
    "Strong PR and earned-media hook with credible data.",
    "Positions Canva as a cultural voice and thought leader beyond templates.",
    "Remixable templates give the audience a participation route."
   ],
   "main_concerns": [
    "Survey-led PR is common and may feel familiar rather than surprising.",
    "Data can feel cold and does not capture the emotion of the milestone.",
    "Methodological scrutiny and a higher production cost."
   ]
  }
 ],
 "selection": {
  "selected_id": "C3",
  "reasons_for_selection": [
   "It rests on the sharpest insight of the four: the memory of a first paycheck is the thing made, not the sum received.",
   "It is the idea most naturally owned by Canva, since the proof is a design, and it needs no forced link between brand and topic.",
   "It is platformable: a repeatable template, a gallery, out-of-home, creator seeding and an employer version can all extend from the same 'First Paid' receipt.",
   "It carries B2B credibility by showing real livelihoods and small-business starts, and it can later feed case studies and sales stories.",
   "Its participation mechanic is simple and emotionally rewarding, which suits Canva's low-friction creation model."
  ],
  "rejected": [
   {
    "id": "C1",
    "reason": "Strong B2B fit but a narrow, employer-driven reach with little cultural spark. Its best element, celebrating a new hire's first paid work, is absorbed into the selected idea as a team version."
   },
   {
    "id": "C2",
    "reason": "Useful but product-led and narrow, and invoicing is a crowded space with specialist competitors. It would drive utility without building emotional meaning around the topic."
   },
   {
    "id": "C4",
    "reason": "Credible and PR-friendly but a familiar survey-and-data format that does not capture the emotion of the milestone. It is also more costly and open to methodological challenge."
   }
  ]
 },
 "revisions_after_selection": [
  {
   "change": "Blur the amount by default and replace it with an optional 'hours it took' or 'what it meant' field.",
   "reason": "Removes the privacy and comparison risks, and keeps the focus on the work and the feeling rather than the salary."
  },
  {
   "change": "Broaden 'the design that earned it' to 'the first thing you made that got paid', including decks, résumés, reports, social posts and menus.",
   "reason": "Not every first paycheck came from a pure design job, and the narrow version would exclude many employees and students."
  },
  {
   "change": "Seed the gallery before launch with creators, small business owners and Canva's own employees, and add a team version where employers can publish a new hire's first paid project.",
   "reason": "Avoids an empty gallery at launch and brings in the B2B and HR audience from C1 without making the idea employer-led."
  },
  {
   "change": "Make the 'First Paid' receipt a reusable template with a simple, ownable visual format that works as a social post, a printable frame and an out-of-home poster.",
   "reason": "A consistent format makes the campaign recognizable at scale and lets it live across channels."
  }
 ],
 "final_proposal": {
  "title": "First Paid",
  "idea": "Nobody remembers the exact amount of their first paycheck. Everyone remembers what they made to earn it: the bakery menu, the pitch deck, the wedding invitation, the logo for a friend's startup. First Paid turns that memory into a shareable 'receipt': a Canva template that pairs the work that earned your first money with the date, who paid you, and a blurred amount. Anyone can add what it meant to them. We seed a public gallery with creators, freelancers and small business owners, and show the receipts as framed pay stubs on posters and feeds. Employers can use a team version to celebrate a new hire's first paid project. The campaign celebrates the start of careers and quietly proves that much of it began in Canva.",
  "follow_up_questions": [
   "What is the most surprising or humble 'first paid thing' you or someone you know made, and what would it say about how careers really start?",
   "How might the 'First Paid' receipt change across cultures where the first paycheck goes to parents or family rather than to the person who earned it?",
   "Beyond the gallery, where could real receipts appear in the world (on shop walls, in company lobbies, in portfolio sites) so the campaign feels like a lasting ritual and not just a moment?"
  ]
 }
}

Return only JSON: {"O": <1-9>, "C": <1-9>, "E": <1-9>, "A": <1-9>, "N": <1-9>, "notes": "<one or two sentences>"}

